1
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:12,240
So he prayed there, much-enduring noble Odysseus,
while the mules' strength carried the girl to the city.

2
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:17,540
And when she reached her father's famous house,
she halted at the gateway; and her brothers around her

3
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:23,400
stood, men like the immortals, and from under the wagon
they loosed the mules, and carried the clothing in;

4
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:26,580
and she herself went to her room. And her fire was lit

5
00:00:26,580 --> 00:00:33,180
by an old woman of Apeire, the chamber-keeper Eurymedusa,
whom once the curved ships brought from Apeire:

6
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:36,140
they had picked her out as a prize of honor for Alcinous,

7
00:00:36,140 --> 00:00:41,080
because he ruled all the Phaeacians,
and the people listened to him as to a god.

8
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:43,980
She had nursed white-armed Nausicaa in the halls,

9
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:48,240
and now she kindled her fire
and made her supper in her room.

10
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:51,900
And then Odysseus rose to go to the city; and Athena,

11
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:55,540
with kind thought for Odysseus,
poured a thick mist around him,

12
00:00:55,540 --> 00:01:02,240
so that no great-hearted Phaeacian, crossing his path,
might needle him with words and ask him who he was.

13
00:01:02,240 --> 00:01:08,360
But when he was on the point of entering the lovely city,
there the goddess, gray-eyed Athena, met him,

14
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:12,340
in the likeness of a young girl,
unwed, carrying a pitcher.

15
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:16,020
She stopped in front of him, and noble Odysseus asked her:

16
00:01:16,020 --> 00:01:21,100
"Child, would you not guide me to the house of a
man named Alcinous, who is lord among these people?

17
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:27,460
I am a stranger, hard-worn, come here from far away,
out of a distant country: so I know not one

18
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:30,020
of the people whose city and land this is."

19
00:01:30,020 --> 00:01:33,020
Then the goddess spoke to him again, gray-eyed Athena:

20
00:01:33,020 --> 00:01:36,020
"Then I will show you,
father stranger, the house you bid me

21
00:01:36,020 --> 00:01:39,980
show: for he lives close by my own blameless father.

22
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:43,160
Only go in silence, so, and I will lead the way:

23
00:01:43,160 --> 00:01:46,600
do not look at any of the people, and ask no questions.

24
00:01:46,600 --> 00:01:52,440
For these men do not much endure strangers,
nor welcome with open love the man who comes from elsewhere.

25
00:01:52,440 --> 00:01:55,840
They trust instead in their fast ships, their swift ones,

26
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:59,420
and cross the great gulf on them,
since the earth-shaker granted it:

27
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:02,480
ships swift as a wing, or as a thought."

28
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:08,320
So speaking, Pallas Athena led the way swiftly,
and he followed in the footsteps of the god.

29
00:02:08,320 --> 00:02:12,780
And the Phaeacians, famed for ships, never marked
him going through the city among them: Athena

30
00:02:12,780 --> 00:02:15,440
of the lovely braids would not
allow it — the dread goddess —

31
00:02:15,440 --> 00:02:20,060
who poured a wondrous haze around him,
with kind thought in her heart.

32
00:02:20,060 --> 00:02:23,260
And Odysseus wondered at the harbors and the trim ships,

33
00:02:23,260 --> 00:02:26,380
at the gathering-places of the
heroes themselves, and the walls,

34
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:30,200
long and high, fitted with palisades, a wonder to see.

35
00:02:30,200 --> 00:02:36,180
But when they came to the king's famous house,
the goddess, gray-eyed Athena, began to speak among them:

36
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:41,160
"Here, father stranger, is the house you bid me
show you. You will find the Zeus-nurtured kings

37
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:46,780
feasting at the feast. Go in, and let your heart
fear nothing: the bold man proves the better man

38
00:02:46,780 --> 00:02:50,000
in every venture, though he come from another country.

39
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,800
First you will come upon the mistress in the halls:

40
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:55,420
Arete is the name she is called, and she comes of the same

41
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:59,080
parents, the very ones who fathered King Alcinous.

42
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:04,200
Nausithous first was born from Poseidon the
earth-shaker and Periboea, loveliest of women,

43
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:08,870
youngest daughter of great-hearted Eurymedon,
who once was king over the overweening Giants —

44
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:13,200
but he destroyed his reckless people,
and was destroyed himself.

45
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:19,520
With her Poseidon lay, and fathered a son,
great-hearted Nausithous, who ruled among the Phaeacians;

46
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,260
and Nausithous fathered Rhexenor and Alcinous.

47
00:03:23,260 --> 00:03:26,480
Rhexenor — still without a son, a bridegroom in his hall —

48
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:32,900
Apollo of the silver bow struck down, leaving one
only child, Arete. Her Alcinous made his wife,

49
00:03:32,900 --> 00:03:35,400
and honored her as no other woman on earth is honored,

50
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:38,800
of all the wives who now keep house under their husbands:

51
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:44,800
so heartfelt is the honor she has held, and holds still,
from her dear children and from Alcinous himself

52
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:47,860
and from the people, who look on her as on a god

53
00:03:47,860 --> 00:03:50,980
and greet her with blessings
when she walks through the town.

54
00:03:50,980 --> 00:03:53,920
For she herself has no lack of noble understanding:

55
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:57,800
for those she favors — even men — she dissolves quarrels.

56
00:03:57,800 --> 00:04:00,440
If she once takes kindly thought for you in her heart,

57
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:03,020
then there is hope you will see your own people, and come

58
00:04:03,020 --> 00:04:05,930
to your high-roofed house and your own fatherland."

59
00:04:05,930 --> 00:04:11,780
So speaking, gray-eyed Athena went away over
the barren sea, and left lovely Scheria,

60
00:04:11,780 --> 00:04:18,440
and came to Marathon and wide-wayed Athens,
and entered the close-built house of Erechtheus. But Odysseus

61
00:04:18,440 --> 00:04:21,060
went toward the famous house of Alcinous, and his heart

62
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:25,180
was churning as he stood there,
before he reached the threshold of bronze.

63
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:32,840
For there was a sheen as of the sun or the moon through
the high-roofed house of great-hearted Alcinous.

64
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:35,400
Walls of bronze ran this way and that,

65
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:41,000
from the threshold to the inmost room,
with a frieze of blue enamel round them;

66
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,960
doors of gold closed the close-built house within;

67
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:49,060
the doorposts standing on the
bronze threshold were of silver,

68
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:55,960
the lintel above was silver, and the door-handle gold;
and on either side stood dogs of gold and silver

69
00:04:55,960 --> 00:05:02,160
that Hephaestus had made with his knowing craft
to guard the house of great-hearted Alcinous —

70
00:05:02,160 --> 00:05:06,340
creatures deathless and unaging all their days.

71
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:10,080
And within, seats stood along the wall this way and that,

72
00:05:10,080 --> 00:05:14,240
from the threshold to the inmost room
in an unbroken line, and over them

73
00:05:14,240 --> 00:05:18,640
were thrown fine close-spun coverings, the work of women.

74
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:24,180
There the leaders of the Phaeacians would sit
drinking and eating: what they had never failed.

75
00:05:24,180 --> 00:05:30,640
And youths of gold on well-built pedestals
stood holding blazing torches in their hands,

76
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:33,400
lighting the nights through the house for the feasters.

77
00:05:33,400 --> 00:05:36,040
And fifty slave-women serve the house:

78
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:41,860
some grind the apple-gold grain at the mill,
others weave at the looms and turn the spindles,

79
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:48,300
sitting close as the leaves of a tall poplar,
and from the close-woven linen the soft oil runs.

80
00:05:48,300 --> 00:05:51,720
As far as the Phaeacian men are skilled beyond all others

81
00:05:51,720 --> 00:05:55,140
at driving a fast ship on the sea, so are their women

82
00:05:55,140 --> 00:06:02,280
masters of the loom: for Athena has given them beyond
others knowledge of beautiful work, and noble minds.

83
00:06:02,280 --> 00:06:06,000
And outside the courtyard,
close by the doors, is a great orchard

84
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,080
of four acres, with a hedge driven round it on both sides.

85
00:06:10,080 --> 00:06:12,940
There tall trees grow in full leaf and flower:

86
00:06:12,940 --> 00:06:19,400
pears and pomegranates and apple trees bright with
fruit, sweet figs and olives in full flourish.

87
00:06:19,400 --> 00:06:25,380
Their fruit never perishes, never fails,
winter or summer, the year round; but always

88
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:29,200
the breathing West Wind sets some growing, ripens others:

89
00:06:29,200 --> 00:06:35,200
pear grows old upon pear, apple upon apple,
grape-cluster upon cluster, fig upon fig.

90
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:41,460
And there his vineyard, heavy with fruit, is rooted,
one part of it a drying-ground on level open earth,

91
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:44,540
baking in the sun; other grapes they are gathering,

92
00:06:44,540 --> 00:06:48,360
and others they are treading;
and out in front are the unripe ones,

93
00:06:48,360 --> 00:06:52,400
one row shedding its blossom,
another darkening toward purple.

94
00:06:52,400 --> 00:06:55,420
And there, by the last row of vines, grow ordered beds

95
00:06:55,420 --> 00:06:59,040
of greens of every kind, shining the whole year through;

96
00:06:59,040 --> 00:07:02,020
and two springs: one is scattered through all the garden,

97
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:04,620
the other dives under the courtyard threshold and runs

98
00:07:04,620 --> 00:07:08,420
toward the high house,
where the townsfolk drew their water.

99
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:12,600
Such were the shining gifts of
the gods in the house of Alcinous.

100
00:07:12,600 --> 00:07:15,860
There much-enduring noble Odysseus stood gazing.

101
00:07:15,860 --> 00:07:21,420
And when his heart had wondered at it all,
he stepped quickly over the threshold into the house.

102
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:23,840
He found the leaders and counselors of the Phaeacians

103
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:27,360
pouring from their cups to the sharp-eyed slayer of Argus,

104
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:30,820
to whom they poured last of all,
when they thought of sleep.

105
00:07:30,820 --> 00:07:34,060
But much-enduring noble Odysseus went on through the house

106
00:07:34,060 --> 00:07:40,580
wrapped in the thick mist Athena had poured around him,
until he came to Arete and to King Alcinous.

107
00:07:40,580 --> 00:07:43,440
And Odysseus threw his arms around Arete's knees —

108
00:07:43,440 --> 00:07:47,420
and then, just then,
the god-sent mist poured back off him.

109
00:07:47,420 --> 00:07:50,060
And they fell dumb through the
house at the sight of the man,

110
00:07:50,060 --> 00:07:54,400
and stared in wonder. And Odysseus made his plea:

111
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:56,620
"Arete, daughter of godlike Rhexenor:

112
00:07:56,620 --> 00:08:00,240
to your husband, to your knees, I come after much labor —

113
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:07,040
and to these feasters. May the gods grant them
fortunate lives; and may each hand down to his children

114
00:08:07,040 --> 00:08:10,580
the wealth in his halls, and the
honors the people have given him.

115
00:08:10,580 --> 00:08:14,440
But for me — speed my sending,
that I may come to my fatherland,

116
00:08:14,440 --> 00:08:18,880
and quickly: for long now,
far from my own people, I suffer pains."

117
00:08:18,880 --> 00:08:24,860
So speaking he sat down on the hearth, in the ashes
by the fire; and they were all hushed in silence.

118
00:08:24,860 --> 00:08:30,300
At long last the old hero Echeneus spoke —
a man elder-born beyond the other Phaeacians,

119
00:08:30,300 --> 00:08:34,060
who excelled in speech, knowing
many things grown old with him;

120
00:08:34,060 --> 00:08:37,920
with good will toward them he spoke to the assembly:

121
00:08:37,920 --> 00:08:41,100
"Alcinous, this is not the
fairer way — it is not fitting —

122
00:08:41,100 --> 00:08:44,000
a stranger sitting on the ground,
on the hearth, in the ashes,

123
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:47,720
while these men hold themselves in, waiting for your word.

124
00:08:47,720 --> 00:08:53,200
Come — raise the stranger up, and seat him on
a chair with silver studs, and bid the heralds

125
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:56,080
mix more wine, so that to Zeus who delights in thunder

126
00:08:56,080 --> 00:09:00,500
we may pour also — the god who
walks beside honored suppliants;

127
00:09:00,500 --> 00:09:04,120
and let the housekeeper give the
stranger supper from her stores."

128
00:09:04,120 --> 00:09:06,300
And when the sacred strength of Alcinous heard it,

129
00:09:06,300 --> 00:09:10,780
he took the hand of battle-wise Odysseus,
the man of shifting counsels,

130
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:17,700
raised him from the hearth and set him on a shining chair,
making his son stand up — kind-hearted Laodamas,

131
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:20,640
who sat beside him, and whom he loved the most.

132
00:09:20,640 --> 00:09:23,280
A handmaid brought water for their hands in a pitcher,

133
00:09:23,280 --> 00:09:26,300
a beautiful golden one, and poured it over a silver basin

134
00:09:26,300 --> 00:09:30,080
for them to wash, and drew up
a polished table beside them.

135
00:09:30,080 --> 00:09:33,540
The grave housekeeper brought
bread and set it before them,

136
00:09:33,540 --> 00:09:37,260
laying out delicacies in plenty,
giving gladly of her store;

137
00:09:37,260 --> 00:09:40,880
and he drank and ate, much-enduring noble Odysseus.

138
00:09:40,880 --> 00:09:44,280
And then the strength of Alcinous said to the herald:

139
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:49,480
"Pontonous, mix the bowl, and serve out the wine to all
in the hall, so that to Zeus who delights in thunder

140
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:53,260
we may pour also — the god who
walks beside honored suppliants."

141
00:09:53,260 --> 00:09:56,920
So he spoke, and Pontonous mixed the honey-hearted wine

142
00:09:56,920 --> 00:10:00,680
and served all, pouring first
drops in each cup for the gods.

143
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:05,520
And drunk what their hearts would have,
Alcinous addressed them all, and said:

144
00:10:05,520 --> 00:10:10,960
"Hear me, leaders and counselors of the Phaeacians,
while I say what the heart in my breast commands.

145
00:10:10,960 --> 00:10:17,000
Now you have feasted: go home and take your rest;
in the morning we will call more of the elders together

146
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:22,960
and host the stranger in the halls, and offer the
gods fine victims; and after that we will think also

147
00:10:22,960 --> 00:10:26,540
of his sending, so that without
labor or pain this stranger

148
00:10:26,540 --> 00:10:33,540
may come, under our escort, to his own fatherland
rejoicing, and quickly, though he come from very far,

149
00:10:33,540 --> 00:10:39,100
and suffer no evil or harm midway before he
sets foot in his own land. There, afterward,

150
00:10:39,100 --> 00:10:42,040
he will suffer whatever Destiny and the grave Spinners

151
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:45,600
spun in his thread at his birth, when his mother bore him.

152
00:10:45,600 --> 00:10:48,640
But if he is one of the deathless ones,
come down from heaven,

153
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:51,400
then this is some new thing the gods are devising:

154
00:10:51,400 --> 00:10:58,540
for always in time past the gods have shown themselves
openly to us, whenever we offer glorious hecatombs,

155
00:10:58,540 --> 00:11:01,620
and they feast beside us, sitting where we sit.

156
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:07,780
And if a lone traveler on the road meets them,
they use no disguise with us — for we are of near kin,

157
00:11:07,780 --> 00:11:11,180
like the Cyclopes and the wild tribes of the Giants."

158
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:14,920
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

159
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:20,220
"Alcinous, let your mind be easy on that count.
I am not like the deathless ones who hold wide heaven,

160
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:23,600
not in build, not in bearing: I am like mortal men.

161
00:11:23,600 --> 00:11:26,040
Whoever you know among mankind who carry the heaviest

162
00:11:26,040 --> 00:11:29,560
loads of misery — it is to them I would compare my pains.

163
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:35,380
Yes, and I could tell a still longer tale of evils,
all that I have labored through by the gods' will.

164
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:37,880
But let me eat my supper, for all my grief:

165
00:11:37,880 --> 00:11:40,900
for there is nothing more
dog-shameless than the hateful belly,

166
00:11:40,900 --> 00:11:46,820
which commands a man to remember it, by force,
however worn he is, however deep the sorrow in his heart —

167
00:11:46,820 --> 00:11:52,000
as I have sorrow in my heart, and still this thing
forever bids me eat and drink, and makes me forget

168
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,320
all I have suffered, and orders me: be filled.

169
00:11:55,320 --> 00:12:01,660
But you — bestir yourselves at the showing of dawn,
to set me, unlucky as I am, on my own fathers' soil,

170
00:12:01,660 --> 00:12:05,220
even after all I have suffered.
And let life leave me once I have seen

171
00:12:05,220 --> 00:12:09,540
my own possessions, my slaves,
and my great high-roofed house."

172
00:12:09,540 --> 00:12:15,740
So he spoke, and they all approved it and urged that
the stranger be sent home, since he had spoken rightly.

173
00:12:15,740 --> 00:12:18,540
But when they had poured,
and drunk what their hearts would have,

174
00:12:18,540 --> 00:12:24,720
the others went home to sleep, each man to his own house,
and noble Odysseus was left behind in the hall,

175
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:30,780
and beside him sat Arete and godlike Alcinous,
while the handmaids cleared away the service of the feast.

176
00:12:30,780 --> 00:12:33,800
And white-armed Arete began to speak among them —

177
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:37,380
for she had recognized the mantle
and the tunic, seeing the beautiful

178
00:12:37,380 --> 00:12:43,260
clothes she herself had made with her attendant women —
and lifting her voice she spoke winged words to him:

179
00:12:43,260 --> 00:12:47,180
"Stranger, this question first, and I ask it myself:

180
00:12:47,180 --> 00:12:50,900
What man are you, and from where?
Who gave you these clothes?

181
00:12:50,900 --> 00:12:53,900
Did you not say you came here wandering over the sea?"

182
00:12:53,900 --> 00:12:56,560
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

183
00:12:56,560 --> 00:12:59,020
"It is hard, my queen, to tell my griefs straight through

184
00:12:59,020 --> 00:13:02,200
to the end: the gods of heaven have given me so many.

185
00:13:02,200 --> 00:13:05,820
But this I will tell you,
since you ask it of me and inquire.

186
00:13:05,820 --> 00:13:12,360
There is an island, Ogygia, lying far off in the sea,
where the daughter of Atlas lives — guileful Calypso

187
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:18,460
of the lovely braids, a dread goddess;
and no one mixes with her, no god, no mortal man.

188
00:13:18,460 --> 00:13:21,700
But me — unlucky me — some power brought to her hearth

189
00:13:21,700 --> 00:13:25,340
alone, after Zeus with a hurl of
the white lightning had shattered

190
00:13:25,340 --> 00:13:28,920
my fast ship apart in the middle of the wine-dark sea.

191
00:13:28,920 --> 00:13:35,080
There all the rest of my brave companions perished,
but I threw my arms round the keel of my curved ship

192
00:13:35,080 --> 00:13:38,520
and was carried nine days;
and on the tenth, in the black night,

193
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:41,720
the gods brought me near the island Ogygia, where Calypso

194
00:13:41,720 --> 00:13:46,280
lives, she of the lovely braids,
the dread goddess. And she took me in

195
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:52,260
and loved me and tended me devotedly, and promised
to make me deathless and unaging all my days;

196
00:13:52,260 --> 00:13:54,920
but she never once persuaded the heart in my breast.

197
00:13:54,920 --> 00:14:01,620
There I stayed seven years unbroken, and forever wet
with my tears the immortal clothing Calypso gave me.

198
00:14:01,620 --> 00:14:07,280
But when the eighth year came wheeling round for me,
then at last she urged me — commanded me — to go,

199
00:14:07,280 --> 00:14:10,900
whether by some message from Zeus,
or her own mind had turned.

200
00:14:10,900 --> 00:14:14,880
She sent me off on a raft bound
plank to plank, and gave me much —

201
00:14:14,880 --> 00:14:18,220
bread and sweet wine —
and dressed me in immortal clothing,

202
00:14:18,220 --> 00:14:21,560
and sent up a fair wind for me, harmless and warm.

203
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:27,180
Seventeen days I sailed on, crossing the water,
and on the eighteenth appeared the shadowy mountains

204
00:14:27,180 --> 00:14:34,080
of your land, and my own heart was glad — ill-fated
as I was: for I was yet to keep company with misery

205
00:14:34,080 --> 00:14:40,500
in plenty, sent upon me by Poseidon the earth-shaker,
who loosed the winds against me and barred my road,

206
00:14:40,500 --> 00:14:43,820
and stirred up a sea past all speaking; nor would the wave

207
00:14:43,820 --> 00:14:47,760
let me be carried on my raft, for all my thick groaning.

208
00:14:47,760 --> 00:14:51,060
The squall then shattered the raft to pieces. But I

209
00:14:51,060 --> 00:14:57,800
swam — cut my way across that gulf — until wind and
water carried me near, and brought me to your land.

210
00:14:57,800 --> 00:15:01,300
There, as I climbed out, the wave
would have forced me onto the shore,

211
00:15:01,300 --> 00:15:06,940
dashing me on the great rocks in a joyless place;
but I gave way, and swam back out, until I came

212
00:15:06,940 --> 00:15:10,260
to a river, where at last the ground seemed best to me,

213
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:13,280
clear of stones, and there was shelter from the wind.

214
00:15:13,280 --> 00:15:17,160
I dropped there, gathering my
spirit back; and ambrosial night

215
00:15:17,160 --> 00:15:20,480
came on. I climbed out clear of the heaven-fed river

216
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:23,620
and fell asleep in the bushes,
and heaped the fallen leaves

217
00:15:23,620 --> 00:15:27,620
around me; and a god poured down boundless sleep.

218
00:15:27,620 --> 00:15:34,020
There in the leaves, my heart worn down with grief,
I slept the whole night through, and dawn, and midday;

219
00:15:34,020 --> 00:15:37,060
the sun was slanting down when sweet sleep let me go.

220
00:15:37,060 --> 00:15:42,760
And I saw your daughter's handmaids on the beach
at play, and she among them, like the goddesses.

221
00:15:42,760 --> 00:15:46,920
To her I made my plea. And she did
not fail in noble understanding —

222
00:15:46,920 --> 00:15:52,540
as you would not hope of the young when you meet them
face to face: for the young are forever thoughtless.

223
00:15:52,540 --> 00:15:59,020
She gave me bread in plenty, and fire-bright wine,
and a bath in the river, and gave me these clothes.

224
00:15:59,020 --> 00:16:03,460
That, for all my grieving,
is the truth of it, told through."

225
00:16:03,460 --> 00:16:06,720
Then Alcinous answered him in turn, and said:

226
00:16:06,720 --> 00:16:09,760
"Stranger, in this one point, truly, my child did not see

227
00:16:09,760 --> 00:16:12,380
rightly: that she did not bring
you with her attendant women

228
00:16:12,380 --> 00:16:15,840
to our house — when she was the first you supplicated."

229
00:16:15,840 --> 00:16:19,100
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

230
00:16:19,100 --> 00:16:22,100
"Hero, do not scold your blameless daughter for that.

231
00:16:22,100 --> 00:16:27,960
She did tell me to follow along with her handmaids;
it was I who would not — out of fear, out of shame,

232
00:16:27,960 --> 00:16:30,480
lest perhaps your own heart darken at the sight:

233
00:16:30,480 --> 00:16:34,260
we are jealous-minded things,
we tribes of men upon the earth."

234
00:16:34,260 --> 00:16:37,020
Then Alcinous answered him in turn, and said:

235
00:16:37,020 --> 00:16:39,040
"Stranger, the heart in my breast is not the kind

236
00:16:39,040 --> 00:16:43,140
to take fire for nothing.
Due measure is better in all things.

237
00:16:43,140 --> 00:16:49,480
If only — father Zeus, Athena, Apollo! —
being the man you are, and thinking as I think,

238
00:16:49,480 --> 00:16:52,160
you would take my daughter and be called my son-in-law,

239
00:16:52,160 --> 00:16:55,220
staying here: I would give you a house and possessions —

240
00:16:55,220 --> 00:17:01,620
if you stayed willingly. Against your will no Phaeacian
shall hold you: father Zeus forbid such a thing.

241
00:17:01,620 --> 00:17:07,820
As for your sending, I appoint it — be sure of it —
for tomorrow. Till then you will lie mastered by sleep,

242
00:17:07,820 --> 00:17:09,840
and they will row the calm, until you come

243
00:17:09,840 --> 00:17:13,820
to your fatherland and your house,
and whatever place is dear to you,

244
00:17:13,820 --> 00:17:19,180
though it be far, much farther than Euboea —
which those of our people say is farthest of all

245
00:17:19,180 --> 00:17:24,900
who saw it, when they carried fair-haired
Rhadamanthus out to visit Tityus, the son of Earth.

246
00:17:24,900 --> 00:17:27,800
They went all the way there,
and finished it without strain,

247
00:17:27,800 --> 00:17:31,180
and on the same day accomplished the journey home again.

248
00:17:31,180 --> 00:17:34,760
And you shall know for yourself,
in your own heart, how far my ships

249
00:17:34,760 --> 00:17:38,980
are the best — and my young men at
throwing up the sea with the oar-blade."

250
00:17:38,980 --> 00:17:46,020
So he spoke; and much-enduring noble Odysseus was glad,
and he spoke in prayer, and said a word, and named:

251
00:17:46,020 --> 00:17:52,600
"Father Zeus, may Alcinous accomplish all he
has said: on the grain-giving earth his fame

252
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:56,500
would be unquenchable —
and I would come to my fatherland."

253
00:17:56,500 --> 00:18:01,880
So the two of them spoke such things to one another,
while white-armed Arete told her handmaids

254
00:18:01,880 --> 00:18:08,580
to set out bedsteads under the porch, and to lay on
them beautiful purple blankets, and spread rugs above,

255
00:18:08,580 --> 00:18:11,600
and fleecy cloaks on top of all, for cover.

256
00:18:11,600 --> 00:18:14,480
Out of the hall they went with torches in their hands;

257
00:18:14,480 --> 00:18:21,000
and when they had briskly spread the close-corded bed,
they came and stood beside Odysseus and called him:

258
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,940
"Up, stranger, come and sleep: your bed is made."

259
00:18:24,940 --> 00:18:28,300
So they spoke; and welcome it seemed to him to sleep.

260
00:18:28,300 --> 00:18:34,720
So he slept there, much-enduring noble Odysseus,
on a corded bedstead under the echoing porch;

261
00:18:34,720 --> 00:18:37,940
but Alcinous lay down in the inner room of the high house,

262
00:18:37,940 --> 00:18:41,018
where the lady his wife made ready his bed and his rest.
